Say whether the configuration thread is ever given a CPU

Run 459 answered the question run 456 opened.  The panel showed

    nio: CFG enq I22AP out=4 wt=3 ct=3
    nio: CFG mk ok t=0xfffffffff2c7728000

So the thread was created: kernel_thread_start returned success and the
handle is a real thread.  All three silent failure paths are ruled out,
the allocation was fine, init was fine, and the thread was made.

And "nio: CFG alive" never appeared.  That line is the first statement in
_IOConfigThread::main, before thread_policy_set and before the wait on
the semaphore, so a thread that ran at all would have printed it.  The
thread exists and its body never ran.

The counters say the bookkeeping is right: wt is 3 and ct is 3, so
pingConfig counted three waiting threads and three configuration threads
and the create condition (gOutstandingJobs > gNumWaitingThreads) was
honoured -- four jobs are outstanding because the fourth thread was just
made.  Nothing is being counted wrong, and nothing was refused.  The
first three configuration threads were made the same way and none of them
ran either.

There are two ways a thread that was made can fail to run, and they need
different fixes:

  * it never reaches the run queue, so the scheduler never sees it;
  * it reaches the run queue and is never given a processor, which is
    what a thread that prefers a cluster that is not powered would do.

This patch marks the hand-off on both sides so that one panel tells them
apart.  The creation side says the thread was made, and the body says it
ran.

The marks are written twice, each in its own way, because the console
cannot be trusted to carry them.  printf goes through console_write(),
which waits for room in the console ring when the ring stops draining, so
a printf can block for ever and say nothing at all -- and run 459 stopped
printing right after the creation line, which is what that looks like.
nio_u_mark() goes through cons_ops[1].putc, which the console blob
replaced with a direct framebuffer writer: no lock, no ring, so it still
writes when the console path is what is stuck.  The matching watchdog
already writes that way from its own thread, so a mark from the
configuration thread is the same kind of thing.

    MKOK    the thread was created            (creation side)
    MKFAIL  the creation failed               (creation side)
    ALIVE   the thread's body was entered      (body side)
    WAIT    the body got to the semaphore      (body side)
    PICK    the body took a job off the queue  (body side)

MKOK with no ALIVE means the thread was made and never ran: the hand to
the scheduler is where it stops.  ALIVE with no WAIT means the body was
entered and stopped before the wait.  WAIT with no PICK means it is
blocked on the semaphore, which is a different problem entirely.

Nothing here changes what the kernel does.  Every addition is a printf or
a mark on a path that already runs.

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
index 59b01d2..77a8268 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
@@ -5423,9 +5423,11 @@ _IOConfigThread::configThread( const char * name )
 		kern_return_t nio_kr = kernel_thread_start(&_IOConfigThread::main, inst, &thread);
 		if (KERN_SUCCESS != nio_kr) {
 			printf("\nnio: CFG mk fail start kr=%d", (int) nio_kr);
+			nio_u_mark("\nMKFAIL");
 			continue;
 		}
 		printf("\nnio: CFG mk ok t=%p", (void *) thread);
+		nio_u_mark("\nMKOK");
 
 		char threadName[MAXTHREADNAMESIZE];
 		snprintf(threadName, sizeof(threadName), "IOConfigThread_'%s'", name);
@@ -6120,6 +6122,7 @@ _IOConfigThread::main(void * arg, wait_result_t result)
 	IOService   *   nub;
 	bool            alive = true;
 	printf("\nnio: CFG alive");
+	nio_u_mark("\nALIVE");
 	kern_return_t   kr;
 	thread_precedence_policy_data_t precedence = { -1 };
 
@@ -6135,7 +6138,9 @@ _IOConfigThread::main(void * arg, wait_result_t result)
 //	randomDelay();
 
 		printf("\nnio: CFG wait");
+		nio_u_mark("\nWAIT");
 		semaphore_wait( gJobsSemaphore );
+		nio_u_mark("\nPICK");
 
 		IOTakeLock( gJobsLock );
 		job = (_IOServiceJob *) gJobs->getFirstObject();
